1
00:00:00,174 --> 00:00:03,065
Hello and welcome back to J D Science.

2
00:00:03,344 --> 00:00:07,036
In today's T-Level Chemistry lesson, we are going to study

3
00:00:07,117 --> 00:00:10,019
A twelve point five, Reversible Reactions.

4
00:00:10,472 --> 00:00:13,851
By the end of this lesson, you should be able to define

5
00:00:13,909 --> 00:00:17,879
dynamic equilibrium, state the conditions it requires,

6
00:00:18,088 --> 00:00:22,454
and use Le Chatelier's principle for concentration, temperature

7
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:23,255
and pressure.

8
00:00:23,708 --> 00:00:27,632
The symbol with arrows both ways means the reaction proceeds

9
00:00:27,690 --> 00:00:28,967
in both directions.

10
00:00:29,362 --> 00:00:31,660
The forward reaction forms products.

11
00:00:31,869 --> 00:00:35,039
The reverse reaction reforms reactants.

12
00:00:35,155 --> 00:00:37,756
Reversible is not the same as equilibrium.

13
00:00:37,930 --> 00:00:40,554
Equilibrium needs specified conditions.

14
00:00:40,797 --> 00:00:44,524
Dynamic equilibrium occurs when a reversible reaction in

15
00:00:44,571 --> 00:00:48,390
a closed system has equal forward and reverse rates, so

16
00:00:48,483 --> 00:00:52,407
macroscopic concentrations or amounts remain constant.

17
00:00:52,581 --> 00:00:54,300
The reactions do not stop.

18
00:00:54,450 --> 00:00:55,437
Both continue.

19
00:00:55,588 --> 00:00:58,990
Constant composition does not mean equal concentrations.

20
00:00:59,164 --> 00:01:00,348
Check the conditions.

21
00:01:00,476 --> 00:01:02,357
The reaction must be reversible.

22
00:01:02,508 --> 00:01:04,075
The system must be closed.

23
00:01:04,284 --> 00:01:07,024
Forward and reverse rates must be equal.

24
00:01:07,198 --> 00:01:09,694
Forward and reverse reactions continue.

25
00:01:09,938 --> 00:01:13,676
Equal reactant and product concentrations are not required.

26
00:01:14,129 --> 00:01:17,763
A sealed flask with equal forward and reverse rates can

27
00:01:17,833 --> 00:01:19,632
be at dynamic equilibrium.

28
00:01:20,085 --> 00:01:23,835
An open vessel that lets a gaseous product escape is not

29
00:01:23,904 --> 00:01:26,493
closed, so it is not at equilibrium.

30
00:01:27,016 --> 00:01:30,975
A sealed flask where the forward rate is greater than the reverse

31
00:01:31,021 --> 00:01:33,007
rate is not at equilibrium yet.

32
00:01:33,622 --> 00:01:37,256
Le Chatelier's principle: when a system at equilibrium

33
00:01:37,337 --> 00:01:41,064
is subjected to a change in conditions, the equilibrium shifts

34
00:01:41,134 --> 00:01:44,117
in the direction that opposes the effect of that change.

35
00:01:44,454 --> 00:01:45,313
Use a method.

36
00:01:45,429 --> 00:01:46,602
Identify the change.

37
00:01:46,683 --> 00:01:48,796
Decide how the system opposes it.

38
00:01:49,005 --> 00:01:50,897
Predict a left or right shift.

39
00:01:51,141 --> 00:01:52,639
State which side is favoured.

40
00:01:52,813 --> 00:01:56,401
Justify using concentration, heat direction, or gas moles.

41
00:01:56,737 --> 00:02:00,580
Concentration, for A plus B in equilibrium with C plus D.

42
00:02:00,789 --> 00:02:03,541
Add A, shift right to use some added A.

43
00:02:03,715 --> 00:02:06,431
Remove A, shift left to form more A.

44
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:08,231
Add C, shift left.

45
00:02:08,382 --> 00:02:10,843
Remove D, shift right to form more D.

46
00:02:11,052 --> 00:02:14,233
Temperature: treat heat as a product or a reactant.

47
00:02:14,442 --> 00:02:18,517
You must identify the exothermic and endothermic directions first.

48
00:02:18,726 --> 00:02:22,487
If the forward reaction is exothermic, write heat on the product

49
00:02:22,534 --> 00:02:22,917
side.

50
00:02:23,068 --> 00:02:26,713
Increasing temperature shifts left, the endothermic reverse.

51
00:02:26,957 --> 00:02:28,791
Decreasing temperature shifts right.

52
00:02:28,942 --> 00:02:32,727
If the forward reaction is endothermic, write heat on the reactant

53
00:02:32,774 --> 00:02:33,180
side.

54
00:02:33,331 --> 00:02:35,304
Increasing temperature shifts right.

55
00:02:35,513 --> 00:02:37,499
Decreasing temperature shifts left.

56
00:02:37,615 --> 00:02:40,447
Increasing temperature does not always shift left.

57
00:02:40,564 --> 00:02:42,665
It depends on the enthalpy direction.

58
00:02:42,816 --> 00:02:46,995
For an exothermic forward reaction, increasing temperature decreases

59
00:02:47,065 --> 00:02:47,901
product yield.

60
00:02:48,145 --> 00:02:51,047
Pressure applies to gaseous equilibria.

61
00:02:51,222 --> 00:02:54,902
Increasing pressure favours the side with fewer moles of gas.

62
00:02:55,181 --> 00:02:58,675
Decreasing pressure favours the side with more moles of gas.

63
00:02:58,919 --> 00:03:02,530
If the gas moles are equal, the equilibrium position does not

64
00:03:02,588 --> 00:03:03,111
shift.

65
00:03:03,447 --> 00:03:05,839
Solids and liquids are not counted.

66
00:03:06,083 --> 00:03:10,065
Nitrogen plus three hydrogen in equilibrium with two ammonia.

67
00:03:10,402 --> 00:03:13,002
Four moles of gas on the left, two on the right.

68
00:03:13,246 --> 00:03:15,150
Increasing pressure shifts right.

69
00:03:15,394 --> 00:03:19,690
Hydrogen plus iodine in equilibrium with two hydrogen iodide.

70
00:03:19,864 --> 00:03:21,861
Two moles of gas on each side.

71
00:03:22,035 --> 00:03:24,322
Increasing pressure causes no shift.

72
00:03:24,531 --> 00:03:28,490
Two sulfur dioxide plus oxygen in equilibrium with two sulfur

73
00:03:28,537 --> 00:03:29,373
trioxide.

74
00:03:29,617 --> 00:03:31,254
Three moles of gas to two.

75
00:03:31,370 --> 00:03:33,331
Increasing pressure shifts right.

76
00:03:33,575 --> 00:03:37,453
If that forward reaction is exothermic, increasing temperature

77
00:03:37,488 --> 00:03:38,370
shifts left.

78
00:03:38,707 --> 00:03:42,329
Calcium carbonate solid in equilibrium with calcium oxide

79
00:03:42,364 --> 00:03:44,500
solid and carbon dioxide gas.

80
00:03:44,895 --> 00:03:47,716
Zero gas moles on the left, one on the right.

81
00:03:47,925 --> 00:03:50,003
Increasing pressure shifts left.

82
00:03:50,398 --> 00:03:52,070
Correct the misconceptions.

83
00:03:52,522 --> 00:03:54,961
Reactions do not stop at equilibrium.

84
00:03:55,135 --> 00:03:57,782
Constant does not mean equal concentrations.

85
00:03:58,177 --> 00:04:01,079
Not every reversible system is at equilibrium.

86
00:04:01,416 --> 00:04:04,017
Increasing pressure does not always shift right.

87
00:04:04,411 --> 00:04:07,326
Increasing temperature does not always shift left.

88
00:04:07,778 --> 00:04:09,264
Examination-style question.

89
00:04:09,417 --> 00:04:10,369
Part a.

90
00:04:10,520 --> 00:04:14,189
Define dynamic equilibrium and state one required condition.

91
00:04:14,224 --> 00:04:15,059
Three marks.

92
00:04:15,396 --> 00:04:19,053
Forward and reverse reactions continue at equal rates in a closed

93
00:04:19,076 --> 00:04:19,622
system.

94
00:04:19,959 --> 00:04:20,563
Part b.

95
00:04:20,772 --> 00:04:24,881
For nitrogen, hydrogen and ammonia, state the effect of increasing

96
00:04:24,939 --> 00:04:25,438
pressure.

97
00:04:25,775 --> 00:04:26,541
Two marks.

98
00:04:26,692 --> 00:04:30,442
The equilibrium shifts right because there are two moles of gas against

99
00:04:30,512 --> 00:04:30,953
four.

100
00:04:31,348 --> 00:04:32,044
Part c.

101
00:04:32,172 --> 00:04:34,216
The forward reaction is exothermic.

102
00:04:34,494 --> 00:04:37,582
What happens to product yield if temperature increases?

103
00:04:37,698 --> 00:04:38,523
Two marks.

104
00:04:38,639 --> 00:04:42,435
The equilibrium shifts in the endothermic reverse direction, so

105
00:04:42,528 --> 00:04:44,049
product yield decreases.

106
00:04:44,571 --> 00:04:45,256
Part d.

107
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:47,880
What happens if you add a reactant?

108
00:04:48,159 --> 00:04:48,948
Two marks.

109
00:04:49,065 --> 00:04:52,861
The equilibrium shifts to use some of the added reactant, so

110
00:04:52,954 --> 00:04:54,057
products are favoured.

111
00:04:54,510 --> 00:04:56,844
Before we finish, complete the model.

112
00:04:57,018 --> 00:05:00,965
Dynamic equilibrium is a closed reversible system with equal

113
00:05:01,035 --> 00:05:03,740
continuing rates and constant composition.

114
00:05:03,914 --> 00:05:08,000
Le Chatelier's principle predicts the opposing shift for concentration,

115
00:05:08,082 --> 00:05:09,579
temperature and pressure.

116
00:05:10,288 --> 00:05:13,863
If you found this useful, please subscribe to J D Science, and

117
00:05:13,921 --> 00:05:18,298
visit j d science dot co dot u k for more explanations,

118
